      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around a flour mill powered by the North Raccoon River, which operated from 1856 until its destruction in 1913.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adel%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A narrow‑gauge railroad reached Adel in the 1870s, later rebuilt as standard gauge by the Milwaukee Road, and now forms part of the Raccoon River Valley Trail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adel%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Adel hosts annual events like the Sweet Corn Festival in August and the Special Olympics Car Show in June, drawing regional visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
